--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Test subcycle time stepping

2. Grid functions

"iteration" is stamped with 100 + 10*cctk_iteration + cctk_level at INITIAL
and at EVOL, so its value on each level records the last pass on which that
level was evolved.

"postrestrict" (restrict="no") is stamped the same way at INITIAL and at
CCTK_POSTRESTRICT. Restriction never overwrites it, so its value on each
level records the last pass on which that level was inside the active range
of a CCTK_POSTRESTRICT traversal. The driver only enters the restriction
phase when the finest level has caught up with its parent, so with 2 levels
and subcycling the level-1 stamp is unchanged on odd passes (1, 3, ...) and
both levels update on even passes (2, 4, ...). If the restriction phase ever
runs on a pass where nothing is time-aligned, the finest level's stamp
changes on an odd pass and the test fails.

With 3 levels (test/subcycle_3levels.par, two coarse steps) the finest level
aligns with level 1 on every second pass and with level 0 on every fourth,
so within each coarse step the "postrestrict" stamps update as follows:

  pass 1: none          (levels at t = 1, 1/2, 1/4)
  pass 2: levels 1, 2   (levels at t = 1, 1/2, 1/2; level 2 restricted into 1)
  pass 3: none          (levels at t = 1, 1, 3/4)
  pass 4: levels 0,1,2  (levels at t = 1, 1, 1; full cascade)

Pass 2 is the case a too-eager gate would get wrong: level 0 must keep its
previous stamp while levels 1 and 2 update.

"prerestrict" (restrict="no") is stamped the same way at INITIAL and in the
driver's CarpetX_PreRestrict group, which CarpetX traverses before the
reflux and the restriction, over the same widened level range as
CCTK_POSTRESTRICT. Its stamps must therefore match "postrestrict" on every
pass; a difference means the hook and the restriction have drifted apart.

"tail_context" (checkpoint="no") holds on each level the context of the last
CCTK_POSTSTEP traversal whose window covered the level: tail_iteration,
tail_time and tail_timefac are that traversal's cctk_iteration, cctk_time and
cctk_timefac, and tail_postrestrict is the value of "postrestrict" it saw (a
canary: it shows that the window came after that iteration's
CCTK_POSTRESTRICT). It is stamped everywhere, so it needs no SYNC, and after
a restart it is also stamped in CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES.
"analysis_context" is its CCTK_ANALYSIS twin (analysis_iteration,
analysis_postrestrict).

Under subcycling the driver runs CCTK_POSTSTEP and CCTK_ANALYSIS once per
iteration, after the batch that steps the finest level and its
CCTK_POSTRESTRICT, over that batch's window: the finest level, widened down
to every coarser level that now shares its clock. These are the levels the
iteration finalized. The window sees the finest level's context: cctk_time =
cctk_delta_time * the shared clock and cctk_timefac = 2^(finest level). Both
stamps are end-of-step results (written in CCTK_POSTSTEP or CCTK_ANALYSIS,
never in the evolution part of a step), so on a level that is mid-step (its
clock differs from the finest level's) they are invalid, and poisoned in the
output, from its CCTK_EVOL until its next end of step. With 3 levels
(test/subcycle_3levels.par, where cctk_delta_time = 1), writing "-" for
invalid and pr(it,L) = 100 + 10*it + L for the postrestrict canary, the
first coarse step gives:

  pass  window  tail_iteration     tail_time         tail_postrestrict
                L0   L1   L2       L0   L1   L2      L0       L1       L2
  1     [2,3)   -    -    1        -    -    1/4     -        -        pr(0,2)
  2     [1,3)   -    2    2        -    1/2  1/2     -        pr(2,1)  pr(2,2)
  3     [2,3)   -    -    3        -    -    3/4     -        -        pr(2,2)
  4     [0,3)   4    4    4        1    1    1       pr(4,0)  pr(4,1)  pr(4,2)

tail_timefac is 4 wherever the stamp is valid, and analysis_context matches
tail_context. Each level's valid tail_iteration values are the iterations
that finalize it: level 0 once, level 1 twice and level 2 four times per
coarse step, each after the restriction that completes the level's step. The
second coarse step repeats this with iterations 5 to 8 and times larger by 1.
Initialization runs CCTK_POSTSTEP once over every level, with the finest
level's context as at every aligned pass, so it stamps every level with
(0,0,4) and pr(0,L).

"step_counts" (grid scalars, checkpoint="no") count every traversal of
CCTK_POSTSTEP (step_count_poststep) and CCTK_ANALYSIS (step_count_analysis),
the one after the initial data included. Each runs once per iteration, so
after iteration n of a fresh run both counts are n + 1. They are not
checkpointed, so that the frozen checkpoints of the recover tests, which
predate them, still recover; CCTK_RECOVER_VARIABLES zeroes them, so after a
restart they count the traversals since the restart.

After a restart, recovery traverses CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES once per
clock group (a run of adjacent levels that share a clock), coarse to fine.
Each traversal covers the group's levels and sees the context of the last
window that covered them. Only the finest group was finalized when the
checkpoint was written, so after the traversal of every other group the
driver invalidates the end-of-step results on its levels. After recovery
every level therefore holds what the uninterrupted run holds after the
checkpointed pass: the finest group's stamps, and invalid stamps elsewhere.
Restarting the 3-level run from each pass of the second coarse step gives
these traversals, each with its window and its (iteration, time, timefac);
"(invalidated)" marks the groups whose stamps do not survive:

  from iteration 5 (clocks 2, 3/2, 5/4): [0,1) (5,2,1) (invalidated)
                                         [1,2) (5,3/2,2) (invalidated)
                                         [2,3) (5,5/4,4)
  from iteration 6 (clocks 2, 3/2, 3/2): [0,1) (5,2,1) (invalidated)
                                         [1,3) (6,3/2,4)
  from iteration 7 (clocks 2, 2, 7/4):   [0,2) (7,2,2) (invalidated)
                                         [2,3) (7,7/4,4)
  from iteration 8 (clocks 2, 2, 2):     [0,3) (8,2,4)

The recover tests in TestSubcyclingMC2/test,
recover-subcycling-openpmd-3clocks.par and -4clocks.par, output such stamps
after restarts from iterations 5 and 9; their references equal the
uninterrupted run's (checkpoint-subcycling-openpmd-3clocks and -4clocks),
invalid entries included. recover-subcycling-openpmd-addlevel.par does the
same after a restart from iteration 6, right after a regrid added a fourth
level (clocks 2, 3/2, 3/2, 11/8): level 0 last stepped in iteration 5,
before level 3 existed, so its traversal [0,1) sees iteration 5, where the
clocks alone would give 4 (the log shows it; its stamps are invalidated).
recover-subcycling-silo-3clocks.par repeats the 3-clock restart from a Silo
checkpoint, in relaxed recovery mode; its references equal those of
recover-subcycling-openpmd-3clocks. recover-subcycling-openpmd-iteration0.par
restarts from the initial checkpoint (IO::checkpoint_ID), where every clock
is 0: one traversal [0,3) (0,0,4), the initialization stamp, and its
references, iteration 0 included, equal the uninterrupted run's
(checkpoint-subcycling-openpmd-iteration0) except for recovery_counts.

"recovery_counts" (grid scalars, checkpoint="no") count the traversals of
CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES (count_post_recover), CCTK_POSTSTEP
(count_poststep), CCTK_ANALYSIS (count_analysis), CarpetX_PreRestrict
(count_prerestrict) and CCTK_POSTRESTRICT (count_postrestrict) during
recovery. CCTK_RECOVER_VARIABLES zeroes them and turns counting on, and
CCTK_CPINITIAL turns it off, so the first output after a restart shows which
bins recovery ran: count_post_recover equals the number of clock groups and
the other counts are 0, since recovery does not rerun end-of-step bins. A
fresh start zeroes them at CCTK_INITIAL with counting off, so they stay 0.
The recover tests in TestSubcyclingMC2/test output them:
recover-subcycling-openpmd-nonrhs.par (two clock groups), -3clocks.par
(three), -4clocks.par (four), -addlevel.par (three), -iteration0.par (one)
and recover-subcycling-silo-3clocks.par (three).
